issue_context.body:
**Describe the bug**
The prototypical `eventfd()` pattern is to call `eventfd_read(fd, &val)` in one thread and call `eventfd_write(fd, val)` from another thread to "wake up" the read thread.

However, in Zephyr, this results in a deadlock.

The root cause is the fdtable lock [here](https://github.com/zephyrproject-rtos/zephyr/blob/main/include/zephyr/posix/sys/eventfd.h#L89).

Please also mention any information which could help others to understand
the problem you're facing:
 - What target platform are you using? any tier0 platform such as `qemu_x86_64`, `qemu_riscv64`, or `mps2_an385`
 - What have you tried to diagnose or workaround this issue? I wrote a testcase. The workaround is to use `poll()`, but that is a pain.
 - Is this a regression? No, it has always been a bug.

**To Reproduce**
Steps to reproduce the behavior:
1. check out https://github.com/zephyrproject-rtos/zephyr/pull/58406 (or `main` if merged)
2. apply the patch below
3. `west build -p auto -b qemu_riscv64 -t run tests/posix/eventfd`
4. Wait forever

This will also time out when run with twister, so alternatively, run
3. `twister -i -p qemu_riscv64 -T tests/posix/eventfd`
4. See error

**Expected behavior**
The prototypical `eventfd()` behaviour should work as expected.

**Impact**
This is a showstopper for my particular use case

**Logs and console output**
```shell
*** Booting Zephyr OS build v3.4.0-rc1-29-g7d71aba0987a ***
Running TESTSUITE eventfd
===================================================================
START - test_overflow
 PASS - test_overflow in 0.002 seconds
===================================================================
START - test_poll_timeout
 PASS - test_poll_timeout in 0.509 seconds
===================================================================
START - test_read_nonblock
 PASS - test_read_nonblock in 0.001 seconds
===================================================================
START - test_read_then_write_block
... 
<hangs forever>
```

**Environment (please complete the following information):**
 - OS: macOS
 - Toolchain: Zephyr SDK v0.16.1
 - Commit SHA or Version used: 5f5b34793dc89a3ee5a33c566333760323e57fa0

**Additional context**
```patch
diff --git a/tests/posix/eventfd/src/blocking.c b/tests/posix/eventfd/src/blocking.c
index 5189cd9aba..2f474b05a3 100644
--- a/tests/posix/eventfd/src/blocking.c
+++ b/tests/posix/eventfd/src/blocking.c
@@ -74,3 +74,30 @@ ZTEST_F(eventfd, test_unset_poll_event_block)
 {
 	eventfd_poll_unset_common(fixture->fd);
 }
+
+K_THREAD_STACK_DEFINE(thread_stack, 1024);
+
+static void thread_fun(void *arg1, void *arg2, void *arg3)
+{
+	eventfd_t value;
+	struct eventfd_fixture *fixture = arg1;
+
+	zassert_ok(eventfd_read(fixture->fd, &value));
+	zassert_equal(value, 42);
+}
+
+ZTEST_F(eventfd, test_read_then_write_block)
+{
+	struct k_thread thread;
+
+	/* k_thread_stack_t *stack, size_t stack_size, k_thread_entry_t entry, void * p1, void * p2, void * p3, int prio, uint32_t options, k_timeout_t delay) */
+	k_thread_create(&thread, thread_stack, 1024, thread_fun, fixture, NULL, NULL, 0, 0, K_NO_WAIT);
+
+	k_msleep(100);
+
+	/* this write never occurs */
+	zassert_ok(eventfd_write(fixture->fd, 42));
+
+	/* unreachable code */
+	k_thread_join(&thread, K_FOREVER);
+}
```

A simple C++ app for Linux demonstrates prototypical behaviour working as expected
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <thread>

#include <sys/eventfd.h>
#include <unistd.h>

int main(int argc, char **argv)
{
  int efd = eventfd(0, 0);
  std::thread th([&]{
        eventfd_t val;
        eventfd_read(efd, &val);
        std::cout << "val is " << val << std::endl;
  });
  sleep(1);
  eventfd_write(efd, 42);
  th.join();
  return 0;
}
```
compile & run:
```shell
g++ -o eventfd-example eventfd-example.cpp
./eventfd-example
val is 42
```
